 [{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.TextBox","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"title","value":"Why MCP Is Awesome: One Protocol to Connect Them All"},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.DateTime","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"publishDate","value":"2026-09-15 09:00:00"},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.TextArea","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"excerpt","value":"Before MCP, every AI integration was a bespoke adapter. One open protocol replaces all that glue code, and it makes assistants far more useful."},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.MediaPicker3","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"listingImage","value":[{"key":"6d1f3c52-8a1e-4c0b-9e0a-3f5b7a2d9c11","mediaKey":"998dd485-9d7c-4fc4-b4fe-d468c26364b1","mediaTypeAlias":"Image","crops":[],"focalPoint":null}]},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.BlockGrid","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"blocks","value":{"layout":{"Umbraco.BlockGrid":[{"$type":"BlockGridLayoutItem","columnSpan":12,"rowSpan":1,"areas":[],"contentKey":"28d98052-0dad-421f-86c5-78f3faad0d76","settingsKey":null},{"$type":"BlockGridLayoutItem","columnSpan":12,"rowSpan":1,"areas":[],"contentKey":"e0878be1-b076-4178-83be-e57c810bbb8d","settingsKey":null},{"$type":"BlockGridLayoutItem","columnSpan":12,"rowSpan":1,"areas":[],"contentKey":"67a4844d-9ea2-45b8-a3f9-0aab45a38a46","settingsKey":null},{"$type":"BlockGridLayoutItem","columnSpan":12,"rowSpan":1,"areas":[],"contentKey":"eebaeae7-e173-4d47-aee8-8594e39c548a","settingsKey":null}]},"contentData":[{"contentTypeKey":"bb7f5adf-fc3c-4f52-a075-0f74693af49d","key":"28d98052-0dad-421f-86c5-78f3faad0d76","values":[{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.RichText","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"content","value":{"markup":"<p>Every team that has wired an AI assistant into their tools knows the pain. One connector for the ticket system, another for the file store, a third for the CMS, and each one is a custom adapter with its own auth, its own schema and its own bugs. Multiply the assistants by the tools and you get a tangle nobody wants to maintain.</p><p>The Model Context Protocol, or MCP, cuts through it. It is an open standard that describes how an assistant discovers what a tool can do and how it calls it. Build a server once and every MCP-aware client can use it.</p>","blocks":{"layout":{},"contentData":[],"settingsData":[],"expose":[]}}}]},{"contentTypeKey":"be4b857e-f79d-48ca-a3be-be71b8705c69","key":"e0878be1-b076-4178-83be-e57c810bbb8d","values":[{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.MediaPicker3","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"image","value":[{"key":"3b9e4d70-2c6a-4f18-a5d3-7e0b1c8f4a52","mediaKey":"045adada-367a-43ef-8aca-e191ff0e7933","mediaTypeAlias":"Image","crops":[],"focalPoint":null}]},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.TextBox","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"caption","value":"Photo: Chris Ried on Unsplash"}]},{"contentTypeKey":"bb7f5adf-fc3c-4f52-a075-0f74693af49d","key":"67a4844d-9ea2-45b8-a3f9-0aab45a38a46","values":[{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.RichText","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"content","value":{"markup":"<p>The pieces are simple. A server advertises tools, each with a name, a description and a typed input schema. A client lists them, hands them to the model, and runs whichever one the model picks. Because the contract is machine-readable, the assistant can work out how to use a tool it has never seen before.</p><p>That also changes who can build integrations. Instead of waiting for a vendor to ship a plugin for your favourite assistant, you expose your own system as an MCP server in an afternoo 

{"name":"Why MCP Is Awesome: Real Work, Not Just Chat","urls":["/why-mcp-is-awesome-real-work-not-just-chat/"],"domains":[],"template":"blogPost"} 
 [{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.TextBox","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"title","value":"Why MCP Is Awesome: Real Work, Not Just Chat"},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.DateTime","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"publishDate","value":"2026-09-29 09:00:00"},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.TextArea","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"excerpt","value":"With MCP an assistant stops describing work and starts doing it: reading your CMS, changing real content and checking its own results."},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.MediaPicker3","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"listingImage","value":[{"key":"a2c8e7b4-5f13-4d96-8b7a-1e4d6c0f2a38","mediaKey":"50cd11f5-cc37-4f2f-ab6f-6ab77bc09228","mediaTypeAlias":"Image","crops":[],"focalPoint":null}]},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.BlockGrid","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"blocks","value":{"layout":{"Umbraco.BlockGrid":[{"$type":"BlockGridLayoutItem","columnSpan":12,"rowSpan":1,"areas":[],"contentKey":"da6f3a35-8bdf-4026-a4d0-b94a25428cf9","settingsKey":null},{"$type":"BlockGridLayoutItem","columnSpan":12,"rowSpan":1,"areas":[],"contentKey":"b6401547-1fcd-46eb-8f65-3ea4bc7907c7","settingsKey":null},{"$type":"BlockGridLayoutItem","columnSpan":12,"rowSpan":1,"areas":[],"contentKey":"5cd0ea19-cab0-4e3c-84c1-754a848056a4","settingsKey":null},{"$type":"BlockGridLayoutItem","columnSpan":12,"rowSpan":1,"areas":[],"contentKey":"61800a05-2c67-446f-ba6a-14d8ee0ab391","settingsKey":null}]},"contentData":[{"contentTypeKey":"bb7f5adf-fc3c-4f52-a075-0f74693af49d","key":"da6f3a35-8bdf-4026-a4d0-b94a25428cf9","values":[{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.RichText","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"content","value":{"markup":"<p>A chatbot that only talks is a clever search box. The moment it can act, it becomes a colleague. MCP is what makes that jump practical, because it gives the assistant a standard way to see your systems and a standard way to change them.</p><p>Take a content management system. Without MCP, an assistant can suggest headlines. With MCP, it can look at your document types, create a page, fill in the fields, attach an image and publish it, all through the same tools a human editor would use.</p>","blocks":{"layout":{},"contentData":[],"settingsData":[],"expose":[]}}}]},{"contentTypeKey":"be4b857e-f79d-48ca-a3be-be71b8705c69","key":"b6401547-1fcd-46eb-8f65-3ea4bc7907c7","values":[{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.MediaPicker3","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"image","value":[{"key":"c7a15f92-6e3d-4b08-9d24-0f8e3a6b5c17","mediaKey":"57de0471-9b3d-427a-82ef-00cbeb577b30","mediaTypeAlias":"Image","crops":[],"focalPoint":null}]},{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.TextBox","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"caption","value":"Photo: Alina Grubnyak on Unsplash"}]},{"contentTypeKey":"bb7f5adf-fc3c-4f52-a075-0f74693af49d","key":"5cd0ea19-cab0-4e3c-84c1-754a848056a4","values":[{"editorAlias":"Umbraco.RichText","culture":null,"segment":null,"alias":"content","value":{"markup":"<p>The best part is the feedback loop. Because the assistant can read as well as write, it can check its own work: create a document type, read it back, notice a missing template and fix it. Mistakes become visible and correctable instead of silently shipping.</p><p>Guardrails matter too. An MCP server decides which tools it exposes, so you can give an assistant exactly the access it needs and nothing more. Editors keep their confirmation prompts for anything destructive, and the humans stay in charge.</p>","blocks":{"layout":{},"contentData":[],"settingsData":[],"ex 

